8.  Glossary of Scripts ..................................................

Script          Hot-key     Key file
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
ATTKPORT.SLC    Ctrl-A      Trader.key

    Attacks ports.  Will not attack if you have less than the amount of
    fighters defined in TLXTW.CFG.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
ATTKSHIP.SLC    Alt-A       Trader.key

    Attacks other ships / deployed fighters.  Uses all available fighters
    when attacking.  If you want to wear the ship down first to try to
    capture it, you should not use this script.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
BBSNAME.SLC     Ctrl-B      Trader.key

    This fills the _entry_name variable in SALT.  _Entry_name is a SALT
    variable that holds the name of the BBS from the dialing directory your
    connected to.

    This serves three purposes:

    It will create directories to store data files for new games.

    If you start Telix while already on line or for some other reason the
    _entry_name variable wasn't passed during log on it will fill that
    variable.

    Another use for this is if you're playing in more than one game one the
    same bbs, you'll need a separate directory to store data files.  Before
    you enter the second game, run this and enter a name to denote the
    change.  Always enter the same name each time you enter that particular
    game.

    This script is normally called by other scripts, but it can be run
    manually (Ctrl-B) if needed, as in the previous paragraph.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUSTED.SLC      Alt-F10     Trader.key

    Normally this will be "called" by other scripts, but it can be run as a
    stand alone if need be.  It writes a data file with the sector #, port #,
    and date as represented by the number of seconds since Jan. 1, 1970 when
    you get busted.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUSTREAD.SLC    F10         Trader.key


    Reads the .dat file, so all you have to do is warp into a sector and
    run BUSTREAD to find the last time you were busted in that sector. If
    the port busts have been cleared then the options to steal or rob the
    port will appear in the display box.  Port bust records are cleared
    every 14 days in the game.

    If the file major.lan exists in the game's directory, then the display
    will show if the port is in a major space lane (MSL).  If you run this
    in a sector without a port, and the file major.lan exists (MAJOR_LN,
    Shift-F1) it will show if that sector is in a major space lane.

    If the file Corp.dat exists in the game's directory, then an extra
    option will appear in the display box.  Corp.dat is an ASCII file that
    you must create, it should contain your handle in the game first,
    followed by your corp team members handles in a column format. For
    example my corp.dat may look like this

    Phil Crown
    team member 1
    team member 2
    up to
    five
    currently

    This option will make keeping track of who's been busted where and when
    effortless.  Currently, this requires that the Corp member is using
    TLXTW Scripts too, but I may try to support a generic format in the
    future.  All you have to do is choose to "R"eceive or "T"ransmit. At
    the end of your turns for the day, run Bustread (F10) and choose the
    Cor"p" option, then "T"ransmit Bust Control records to corp members.
    When a corp member logs on, he runs "R"eceive Bust Control records.
    Now, when you run Bustread or Denscan scripts, then it will show if you
    or a corp member was busted in that sector last.

    Example of the display box:

    ÚÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ¿
    ³ Sector  : 806  Class 9        Major Space Lane ³ - port, class, MSL
    ³ Notes  : stock up here                         ³ - notes
    ³ Busted  16 days 7 hours 42 minutes ago. (2x)   ³ - your last bust/times
    ³ Corp    1 days 3 hours 49 minutes ago.         ³ - corp member busted
    ³ All busts will be cleared in 12 days           ³ - when busts cleared
    ³   Steal Ore  -   Equipment  -  Rob credits     ³ - options
    ³   Sell/Steal Cycle          Attack Port        ³ - "    "
    ³                                                ³
    ³   Density Scan                                 ³ - "    "
    ³   Look up a sector                             ³ - "    "
    ³   Manually Enter a Sector and Port             ³ - "    "
    ³   Corp Bust Control Receive/Transmit           ³ - Exchange Corp busts
    ³                                                ³   records
    ³                                                ³
    ³                                                ³
    ÀÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÙ

    Example of the No"t"es file. (you'll see what I mean when you run this
    feature).  Notes.ttw must be formatted like below;

    |    1 Terra (colonists)
    |  100 my planet
    | 1000 ferrengal
    | ^^^^sector number
    |^-space
    |<-left margin

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CIM_WARP.SLC    Ctrl-L      Trader.key

    Enters CIM (Computer Interrogation Mode) and plots warp paths.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLASS.SLC       Alt-F9

    Prints a box with all the port classes.  Nothing fancy, but maybe
    helpful for anyone learning the classes.

                            ÚÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ¿
                            ³Class 1 BBS³
                            ³Class 2 BSB³
                            ³Class 3 SBB³
                            ³Class 4 SSB³
                            ³Class 5 SBS³
                            ³Class 6 BSS³
                            ³Class 7 SSS³
                            ³Class 8 BBB³
                            ³Class 9 BBB³
                            ÀÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÙ

    If this is run in a sector with a port in it, it will print the
    appropriate 3 letter identifier next to the port.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG.SLC      Alt-E       Trader.key

    Edits the configuration file, TLXTW.CFG.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORPMEMO.SLC    Alt-F8      Trader.key

    Allows non CEO Corporate members to send messages to all other corp
    members by typing the message just one time.

    The file corp.dat must exist in the game's directory.  Corp.dat is
    described with the description for BUSTREAD, (above).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CRAI.SLC        Ctrl-C      Trader.key
CRAI.EXE

    Builds a data (.dat) file from the CIM port (.prt) report file.  If
    you're online run DNINTG.SLC (Ctrl-D) first to download the CIM Port
    report and gather any other reports you want to capture in the log
    file, then run this.  The most important reports to capture in the log
    are the CIM Port report and the Game Configuration and Status ("V"
    screen).

    If you're off line, CRAI can still be run.  There should be a current
    log file with the CIM Port report and the "V" screen with the game's
    age for updating the bust control available.

    Crai.exe will extract the Stardock, Class 0 ports, CIM sector/port, and
    the game's age from the log file.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CRCS.SLC        keypad -    Trader/Planet.key
    Computer report for current sector.  Determines which prompt you're at
    and enters the correct keystrokes for getting a Port Report for the
    Current sector.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CRDTCALC.SLC    Alt-F6      Trader.key

    Citadel Treasury calculator.  Calculates interest accumulated in the
    treasury for a 24 hour period.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CRSEPLOT.SLC    Ctrl-P      Trader.key

    Pops up a box with the current sector, asks for a destination sector,
    then counts turns and fuel needed to Twarp from current sector to a
    destination and back to the current sector and prints the totals to the
    screen and on the status line.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CUSGRIMY.SLC    Shft-F3     Trader.key

    Call's the Grimy Trader a bad name, thus causing your alignment turn
    negative.  Load this at the "Command [TL" prompt and it will show
    exactly what your alignment is at all times.  If you load it in the
    Tavern then it will only show how many points your alignment has gone
    down.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
DENSCAN.SLC     Alt-S or keypad +   Trader.key

    Reads the .dat file, and will show what class port is in an adjacent
    sector when doing a density scan if that sector is not unvisited, and if
    busted then how many days ago.

    If the locations of the three Class 0 and the Class 9 (Stardock) ports
    are recorded in the .dat file, and you've run MAJOR_LN.SLC (Shft-F1),
    then it will show if it is a major space lane or not.

    If the file CORP.DAT exists in the game's directory this will show if
    you or a corp member were busted in the adjacent sector last.  See the
    description for BUSTREAD for more on Corp.dat.  In the above example, I
    was busted in sector 487 10 days ago, and a corp member was busted in
    sector 705 2 days ago.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEPLOY_F.SLC    F1      Trader.key

    Deploys fighters.  Doesn't deploy in Fedspace.  The number of fighters
    and the mode are defined in Tlxtw.cfg.  As well as, whether or not to
    deploy in the Major Space Lanes.  Run Config.slc (Alt-E) the default
    values.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
DNINTG.SLC      Ctrl-D  Trader.key

    Download reports intended to be captured in a log file.  If the log
    file is closed or paused, it will be opened first.

    You should choose to get the CIM Port and View Game Status screen, at
    least, as well as any other information you want to capture.

    The default reports to capture are in the configuration file,
    Tlxtw.cfg, but they can be changed on the fly after the script is
    loaded.

    Reports that can be captured are:

                      R = Trader Rankings
                      O = List Corporations
                      N = Rank Corporations
                      G = Deployed Fighters
                      K = Deployed Mines
                      I = Ship Information
                      C = Corporate Planet Scan
                      E = Personal Planet Scan
                      T = Team Assets
                      V = View Game Status
                      X = Avoids
                      P = Port (CIM)
                      S = Sector (CIM)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
DOS.SLC         Alt-J       Trader.key
DOS.SLT         source file

    Shells to dos, closes the capture file, if its open, and re-opens it
    after returning to Telix.  This is so if you want to edit the capture
    file or run an external utility that may try to read the file.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
ERR90.SLC       Alt-9       Trader.key
ERR90.SLT

    Exits Telix with error level 90, Tl.bat handles the error level, to
    edit TL.bat itself, then return to Telix already Online.
    This is a way to change the DR environment variable on the fly,
    to achieve optimum performance.
    If you move TL.BAT to a different directory after running setup, edit
    tl.bat with your text editor and change the line point to the directory
    where tl.bat resides.  It is commented showing what needs editing.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
ETHEREND.SLC    Ctrl-E  Trader.key

    Fires ether probes.  Reads sector numbers from a text file named
    Ether.123.

    The text file can be an ascii download of the Unexplored sectors
    or it can be a list sector numbers in a column that you can create.
    The second method is the preferred way, since you can select optimized
    and specific target sectors to shoot at.

    If the file Ether.123 does not exist in the game's directory OR in in
    the directory specified by the "Temp" environment variable, this script
    will enter the ship's onboard computer and do an ASCII download of
    unexplored sectors.

    After the download is finished, the script will run the editor to edit
    the file it just downloaded (ether.123). This first line in ether.123
    should read: " <ÄÄ CUT UNTIL THIS ROW CONTAINS SECTORS  ÄÄÄ> ".

    The first row of ether.123 must contain only sector numbers.  Trimming
    off the bottom rows helps too.

    You can choose to watch for Aliens, Traders, Mines, Planets, Ferrengi,
    Class 0 ports, a combination, or nothing at all, however, "Probe
    destroyed" is always watched for.  If one of the above is seen, except
    for Class 0 ports, then it will be automatically saved in the file
    called Ether.img in the game's directory in a file that can be easily
    sorted for easy viewing later.

    If you choose to watch for Class 0 ports, when a probe enters a
    sector with a Class 0 port, then that port will be added to the data
    file automatically, if it is not already recorded.

    There are three options for replenishing probes.  Never, Ask, or Always.
    If you choose Ask or Always, it will (after Asking, if applicable)
    port at the StarDock and buy 25 more ether probes, providing you're in
    the same sector as SD and have enough credits.  If you don't have enough
    credits for 25 probes, it will buy what you can afford.

    A temporary file called Ether.tmp is written that holds a string that
    represents the offset of the last sector read from the text file
    (ether.123) so that if the script is aborted, then resumed it won't
    shoot into sectors that its already done.

    If you want to use a pre-existing text file of sectors, copy the file
    you want to use to the directory specified in the "Temp" environment
    variable and name it ETHER.123.  Ex: d:\tmp\ether.123.  The .dat file
    makes a good list of sectors to shoot at after you've run out of
    unexplored sectors.  Cut out just the sector numbers and copy them to
    a file called Ether.123 in the directory specified by the "Temp"
    environment variable.  You should delete all the files in the
    temporary directory when you are finished.

    If you want to use a "permanent" ether probe file, copy the file
    Ether.123 to the game's directory.  The script will search the game's
    directory first, then the temporary directory.  If Ether.123 is not
    found in either directory then it will do an ASCII download of the
    unexplored sectors.

    Summary:

    Ether.123  (list of sectors to shoot at)
    Ether.Img  (image of anything that the script sees)
    Ether.tmp  (offset of last sector read from ether.123)

    Etherend requires the use of the External DOS Command SORT.EXE.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
EXITTELX.SLC    Alt-X       Trader.key
EXITTELX.SLT

    Exits Telix fast.  Doesn't ask any questions.  Don't use this unless
    you really want to exit Telix.  Does not hang up the modem, if
    online.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
FUEL.SLC        F6          Trader.key

    Robs a port of Ore for T-warp or whatever.  The port must have at least
    at least the number of you holds that you want to steal on its docks.

    If you get busted, Busted (Alt-F10) is called which records the bust in
    the .bus file.  If you get busted after you abort the script, you will
    have to run Alt-F10 to record the bust in the .bus file.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
GAMEDIR.SLC     Ctrl-F1     Trader.key
GAMEDIR.SLT

   Shells to the game's directory and runs List, Qedit, command.com, or
   whatever you want to run.  Edit this script as needed and compile with
   CS.exe.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
GRIMY.SLC       Ctrl-G      Trader.key

    Asks the Grimy Trader to trace another trader.  You must enter enough
    letters to match the trader being traced exactly right the first time
    Grimy says "Do you mean ***?"  Saves any info the Grimy Trader gives in
    a capture file named Grimy.Txt in the game's directory.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
HANGUP.SLC      Alt-H       Trader.key
HANGUP.SLT

    Hangs up the modem and resets the script directory and capture
    filename to the what they were when setup.exe ran.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
HARDPWGE.SLC    Ctrl-F4     Trader.key

    Load this at the <Tavern> prompt and it will ask the grimy trader for
    the password to the underground.
    Load it while at the entrance to the Underground ("Your reply :") and
    it will send the password, if underpas.txt exists.
    You must have enough credits to pay grimy for the password.
    Saves the password in a file called UNDERPAS.TXT in the game's
    directory.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOWBADAM.SLC    Shft-F2     Trader.key

    How many holds / credits are safe to steal.  Looks at your experience,
    divides it by x, multiplies it by y, then prints the results on the
    status line.  x and y are listed in the configuration file, Tlxtw.cfg.
    Run Alt-E or to change the default values.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOADKE.SLC      Alt-F2      Trader.key/Planet.key

    Switches between Planet and Trader.key keyboard definition files.
    Planet.key holds all the planet operations scripts, and Trader.key holds
    everything else.  You can reassign the keyboard anyway you want to, and
    even create new key files if you like.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
MAJOR_LN.SLC    Shft-F1     Trader.key

    Finds the major space lanes.  You must have the three Class 0 ports
    (Terra, Alpha Centuari, and Rylos) and the Class 9 (Stardock) recorded
    in the .dat file and sorted by sector number, ascending.  This script
    will tell you if you don't have all the ports and/or if they need to be
    sorted with CRAI.SLC.

    If all three Class 0 ports or the Class 9 port are not in the dat file,
    but you know their locations, run BUSTREAD.SLC (F10) and "M"anually
    enter them into the data file.  Then run this again.
 
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
MATLMOVE.SLC    F3          Planet.key

    Moves commodities between planets within the same sector.  Make sure
    your HOLDS ARE EMPTY when you start.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
MINE_DIS.SLC    Alt-M       Trader.key

    Launches mine disruptors into the sector you specify.  Aborts when there
    are no more mines in the sector, or if you accidentally start disrupting
    your own mines ;-)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
MOVE.SLC        keypad .    Trader.key

    Move /  Scan.  You have to enter a destination first, then use this to
    single step the rest of the way Upgraded Density Scanning each step of
    the way.
 
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PHOTON.SLC      Alt-P       Trader.key

    Launches a photon-torpedo into the sector you specify then enters the
    sector begins the landing sequence and will Attack ONCE, then you're
    on you're own.  Doesn't actually do the invading.  Just gets into the
    sector fast.  It will ask for a sector, a planet number, and how many
    fighters to use.  If you don't know the planet's number you want to
    invade, just hit enter to skip it, then enter the amount of fighters.

    This script will launch the photon, then print the elapsed wave time,
    and the wave maximum duration on the status line, so you'll know how
    much time you have left before the wave expires.  Press escape or wait
    10 seconds when the script is finished before loading another script.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PLANET.KEY      Alt-F2  Planet.key/Trader.key

    Planet Scripts keyboard template.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PLANET_D.SLC    F5          Planet.key

    Planet Destroyer - blows up planets for experience and fun.  You must
    belong to a Corporation for this to work.  Its a good idea to belong to
    a corp even if you're the only one in it.  This script works best with
    ANSI turned off.  Its written to be run in the sector with the
    Stardock, so when you run out of Genesis torps and/or atomic detonators
    it will port and get some more. It asks for a planet number so that you
    don't accidentally land on a populated planet (if there is one) and get
    blown up yourself, or if you have a Planet Scanner.

    If a text file called PLANETD.NAM exists in the game's directory, it
    will be used to name the planets, otherwise they're randomly named.
    This is just a text file that you create with the names of the planets
    in a column format.  If you use this method, it is best that at least
    one planet exists in the sector already, so you know for sure the
    planet's number to enter when it asks, so that the script will run
    non-stop.

    Planet One
    Planet Two
    Planet Three

    Enter 'c' or 'C' as the planet's number to "Clean up", if there are
    a lot of unsightly planets in the sector.  Warning:  Make sure there are
    no colonists on the planets or you may be blown up.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PLANPORT.SLC    F6          Planet.key
    Buys product from a port, and transfers it to the planet.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PLANSHLD.SLC    F9          Planet.key
    Puts shields on a planet.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PLA_PO_M.SLC    F4          Planet.key
    Planet population mover.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
POPSTNRD.SLC    F2          Planet.key
